{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1774496671,"nonce":"ZXPU9Yq12eAhR92tsm0cMsx3YSo=","title":"Umbrella: memory-efficient training — streaming loss, state_filter, jax.checkpoint","message":"Umbrella for memory optimization during training. Three complementary approaches:\n\n1. **Streaming loss**: Compute loss incrementally inside lax.scan, returning only the accumulated scalar. Eliminates trajectory storage entirely for training. All current loss terms (effector pos/vel, net.hidden, efferent.output) are streamable.\n\n2. **State filter (selective trajectory storage)**: Only store state components needed for non-loss purposes (analysis, visualization). Uses the existing ForgetfulIterator/memory_spec pattern, or a new state_filter parameter on eval_trials.\n\n3. **jax.checkpoint on scan body**: Recompute forward activations during backprop instead of storing 130 copies. Reduces backward-pass memory at ~2x compute cost (negligible for small models).\n\nCombined, these could reduce per-run VRAM from ~18GB to potentially \u003c1GB, enabling 4+ concurrent training runs per 24GB GPU.\n\nThis should integrate with Feedbax Studio — users should be able to control which trajectories are stored via the UI. The streaming vs full-trajectory choice should be a training config option.\n\nCross-ref: rlrmp session 31fa1977 memory optimization analysis. Codex and Opus analyses confirmed these approaches.","files":null}]}